      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate fragment.\n\nCertificate details:\n- Number: R85/2008-GB1-11.01\n- Revision 1\n- Scheme: This is an OIML Certificate of Conformity - scheme... R85 doesn't typically fall under OIML-CS scheme A or B distinction? Actually OIML-CS has Scheme A (with certificate + OIML type evaluation report) and Scheme B (certificate only). R85 certificates... The schema asks scheme (A|B). Hmm. For this cert, no explicit scheme mentioned. I could omit or infer. R85/2008-GB1-11.01 - the format suggests standard OIML certificate. OIML-CS launched in 2017; R85 2008 certs under MAA? Not clear. I'll omit scheme or maybe not include it since not present. Rule 8: don't fabricate. Omit scheme.\n\n- Issuing authority: National Measurement and Regulation Office\n- Address: Stanton Avenue, Teddington, TW11 0JZ, United Kingdom\n- Person responsible: Paul Dixon – Product Certification Manager\n- Tel: +44 (0) 20 8943 7272, Fax..., Web www.gov.uk/nmro\n- Email: not present\n- OIML issuer ID: not explicitly given. UKAS mark 0135? That's UKAS accreditation number. Not OIML issuer ID. Omit.\n\n- Applicant: Dem. G. Spyrides S.A., 24 Athinon Avenue, 10441 Athens, Greece\n- Manufacturer: The applicant → so manufacturer name = \"Dem. G. Spyrides S.A.\"? Or record as \"The applicant\". I think better to record manufacturer name as \"The applicant\" or resolve. Hmm. I'll record name \"The applicant\" — but that's not useful. Actually the manufacturer is the applicant, so I could set manufacturers[0] = same as applicant. But safer to record what's stated. I'll put name: \"Dem. G. Spyrides S.A.\" with note? There's no note field. I'll record name \"The applicant\" to be faithful. Hmm... Actually, given rule to not fabricate, recording \"The applicant\" is literal. But the schema says manufacturers[].name — I could use \"The applicant\". I'll do that.\n\n- Certified type: category — this is R85 (Automatic level gauges for tanks). The certified pattern: \"AMETEK 7100 PSU Controller and AMETEK Petro-Stik probe\". Category: could be \"Automatic tank gauge\" or \"Level gauge\". R85 is \"Automatic level gauges for the storage of liquid petroleum products\". Category: \"Automatic level gauge\" perhaps. Components: Probes (AMETEK Petro-Stik 7100 & 7250, AMETEX 7100V & 7250V Flex-Stik) and Consoles (AMETEK 7100 PSU).\n\nType designations: AMETEK 7100 PSU Controller, AMETEK Petro-Stik probe, and probe types: AMETEK Petro-Stik 7100, 7250, AMETEX 7100V, 7250V Flex-Stik. Console: AMETEK 7100 PSU.\n\nComponents:\n1. role: \"Probe\", type_designations: [\"AMETEK Petro-Stik 7100\", \"AMETEK Petro-Stik 7250\", \"AMETEX 7100V Flex-Stik\", \"AMETEX 7250V Flex-Stik\"]. Hmm — the original text: \"AMETEK Petro-Stik 7100 & 7250, AMETEX 7100V & 7250V Flex- Stik\". So \"AMETEK Petro-Stik 7100 & 7250\" = Petro-Stik 7100 and 7250; \"AMETEX 7100V & 7250V Flex-Stik\" = 7100V and 7250V Flex-Stik. So four designations. Alternatives \"OR\" — these are alternative probe models. I'll set alternatives: \"OR\".\n\nProbe characteristics:\n- measuring_principle: \"Magnetostrictive\" (string value)\n- materials: stainless steel (Petro-Stik), PVDF (Flex-Stik) — could be config_level with axis \"probe type\" — condition: \"Petro-Stik\" → \"Stainless steel\"; \"Flex-Stik\" → \"PVDF\". Attribute: probe_material.\n- length: up to 2.8 m → {min: null, max: 2.8}, unit m\n- fuel_height_accuracy: better than ±1 mm → value \"better than ±1 mm\"? Better: {min: null, max: 1}, unit \"mm\", value should be max absolute error 1 mm. I'll record as value 1, unit mm, maybe attribute fuel_height_accuracy with value {min: -1, max: 1}? The text: \"better than +/- 1 mm\". I'll use value: 1, unit_symbol: \"mm\" with attribute fuel_height_accuracy_max_error? Hmm. Simplest: attribute \"fuel_height_accuracy\", value {min: -1, max: 1}, unit \"mm\". That's a defensible representation of ±1 mm. I'll do that.\n- temperature: -25 °C to +55 °C → {min: -25, max: 55}, unit °C. Attribute: temperature_range (operating).\n\n2. role: \"Console\", type_designations: [\"AMETEK 7100 PSU\"].\nConsole characteristics:\n- display_resolution: 0,1 mm → 0.1 mm\n- ambient_temperature: 0 °C to 50 °C → {min: 0, max: 50}, °C\n- voltage: 230 -15%/+10% → value 230, unit V, footnote? It's nominal 230 V with tolerance -15%/+10%. Record as attribute \"supply_voltage\", value 230, unit V, and maybe a separate note. Could add characteristic \"supply_voltage_tolerance\": \"-15%/+10%\". I'll add both.\n\nThese sub-characteristics go into components[].characteristics as structured values. The schema says components[].characteristics{} — values should follow structured form presumably. I'll use structured value objects.\n\nModel family? The cert covers multiple probe models (7100, 7250, 7100V, 7250V) — could be model_family with models. The probe list is 4 variants. I could include model_family with models for probes. But the \"certified type\" includes both console and probe. I think components capture it. Rule 4: model family for variants. The differences between probe variants: materials (Petro-Stik stainless vs Flex-Stik PVDF). I could emit model_level characteristics for probe material. Let me use config_level for material with axis \"probe type\"? Or model_level with models being probe designations.\n\nSimplest robust: components with role \"Probe\" including characteristics type_level-ish, and a config_level entry for material. Actually the schema for components just says characteristics{} — free-form. I'll include structured values there.\n\nRecommendation: R85, edition 2008, scheme? The certificate number includes scheme info. R85 is an OIML Recommendation; OIML-CS scheme... For R85, certificates are issued under OIML-CS scheme B? Actually, most R certificates pre-CS. The schema asks scheme under recommendation. I don't have explicit scheme; omit.\n\nrecommendation: id \"R85\", edition 2008. Note \"Edition 2008(E)\". accuracy_classes: none.\n\nTest reports: TR0598 (22 pages, role type_evaluation_report? \"test report\"), P00596 checklist (12 pages, role \"documentation_file\"? It's a checklist — maybe role \"test_report\" or \"documentation_file\"). I'll list TR0598 as test_report (the cert calls it \"associated test report\"), and P00596 as documentation_file role? Hmm, \"associated checklist\". I'll use role \"checklist\"? The schema role enum: type_evaluation_report | test_report | documentation_file | evaluation_report. Checklist → closest is documentation_file. I'll use documentation_file for P00596. Date: not given.\n\ntest_reports:\n- {id: \"TR0598\", pages: 22, role: \"test_report\"}\n- {id: \"P00596\", pages: 12, role: \"documentation_file\"}\n\nRevision history:\n- revision \"0\"? First entry: R85/2008-GB1-11.01, 28 June 2011, \"Type approval first issued\". Revision field string — original issue. Use revision \"0\"? The schema example: revision \"0\". First issued — I'd mark revision \"0\" (original). Second: \"Revision 1\", date 20 May 2015, \"Table 1, Flex Stik probes added.\" The table shows \"R85/2008-GB1-11.01 Revision 1-\". I'll use \"1\".\n\nCertificate:\n- number: \"R85/2008-GB1-11.01\"\n- scheme: omit (unknown)\n- project_number: \"TS18/0002\" — Reference No. That's a reference number; I'll put as project_number? It's \"Reference No: TS18/0002\". Could be NMRO reference. I'll include as project_number.\n- page_total: 2\n- member_state: \"United Kingdom\"\n- date_issued: \"20 May 2015\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: omit.\n\nIssuing authority:\n- name: \"National Measurement and Regulation Office\"\n- address_lines: [\"Stanton Avenue\", \"Teddington\", \"TW11 0JZ\", \"United Kingdom\"]\n- person_responsible: \"Paul Dixon\"\n- person_title: \"Product Certification Manager\"\n- phone: \"+44 (0) 20 8943 7272\"\n- website: \"www.gov.uk/nmro\"\n- email: omit\n\nNote: signature by G Stones, Technical Manager - Certification Services. The \"person responsible\" is Paul Dixon. G Stones signed. I'll keep person_responsible as Paul Dixon.\n\nApplicants: [{name: \"Dem. G. Spyrides S.A.\", address_lines: [\"24 Athinon Avenue\", \"10441 Athens\", \"Greece\"]}]\n\nManufacturers: [{name: \"The applicant\"}] — hmm. Maybe better: name \"Dem. G. Spyrides S.A.\" since manufacturer is the applicant. I'll use name: \"The applicant\" literally? The instruction says parse party layouts correctly; \"Manufacturer: The applicant\" means manufacturer = applicant. I think resolving to applicant name is more useful and accurate. I'll set name \"Dem. G. Spyrides S.A.\" with same address. Actually risk of fabricating? It's a direct logical resolution stated in the document. I'll resolve it.\n\nCertified type:\n- category: \"Automatic level gauge\" (R85). I'll say \"Automatic tank level gauge\"? R85 title: \"Automatic level gauges for the storage of liquid petroleum products\". I'll use \"Automatic level gauge\".\n- type_designations: [\"AMETEK 7100 PSU Controller\", \"AMETEK Petro-Stik probe\", \"AMETEK Petro-Stik 7100\", \"AMETEK Petro-Stik 7250\", \"AMETEX 7100V Flex-Stik\", \"AMETEX 7250V Flex-Stik\", \"AMETEK 7100 PSU\"]. Hmm, maybe keep top-level to the pattern identification: \"AMETEK 7100 PSU Controller\", \"AMETEK Petro-Stik probe\". Components carry the full model list. I'll include the pattern-level designations plus maybe the console \"AMETEK 7100 PSU\". Keep top-level: [\"AMETEK 7100 PSU Controller\", \"AMETEK Petro-Stik probe\"].\n- module_designation: null / omit. R85 doesn't use modules. Omit.\n- description: from identification: \"AMETEK 7100 PSU Controller and AMETEK Petro-Stik probe\" — that's the pattern. Description could include the R85 context. I'll put description: \"Automatic level gauge consisting of AMETEK 7100 PSU Controller and AMETEK Petro-Stik probe\". Hmm — avoid fabricating. I'll keep description minimal or use the probes/console info. I'll include a description summarizing Table 1: probes magnetostrictive, console display. Maybe simpler to omit description since the identification line is captured in type_designations. I'll include description: \"AMETEK 7100 PSU Controller and AMETEK Petro-Stik probe\". That's fine.\n\nOptional features: \"Connection to a remote PC (as the indicating device) utilising 'Tank Guard' software as an alternative to a display.\" — optional_features not in schema; could fold into description. I'll add to description or matrix_tables? Not a table. I'll append to certified_type.description.\n\nModel family: should I include? Probes: 4 variants with material attribute. I'll include model_family with models for the four probes? But certified type covers console+probe combos. Maybe simpler: config_level for material. I'll skip model_family since variant structure is captured in components. Actually rule 4 says include ONLY if cert covers >1 model variant. It does cover 4 probe variants... but attributes don't vary numerically per model except material. The materials line \"Stainless steel – Petro-Stik, PVDF – Flex-Stik\" varies by probe type. I could emit model_level entry:\n\nattribute: \"probe_material\", values: [{model: \"Petro-Stik 7100\", value: \"Stainless steel\"}, ...]. Hmm but value must be structured {value, unit_symbol, unit_id}. In model_level the values follow structured form.\n\nI think a reasonable approach: model_family.family_name null, models: probe models? But consoles only one model. Mixed. I'll instead put material variation as config_level within the probe component: axis \"probe type\", values: [{condition: \"Petro-Stik (7100 & 7250)\", value: \"Stainless steel\"}, {condition: \"Flex-Stik (7100V & 7250V)\", value: \"PVDF\"}]. attribute: \"wetted_material\" or \"probe_material\".\n\nI'll put these in components[0].characteristics as config_level-like entries? The component characteristics{} is free-form; I'll structure it as:\n\n\"characteristics\": {\n  \"measuring_principle\": {value: \"Magnetostrictive\", unit_symbol: null, unit_id: null},\n  \"probe_material\": config_level? Nested config_level inside component characteristics is odd. Alternatively component characteristics keys directly with condition lists.\n\nI'll do: component probe characteristics:\n{\n \"measuring_principle\": {\"value\": \"Magnetostrictive\", \"unit_symbol\": null},\n \"length\": {\"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 2.8}, \"unit_symbol\": \"m\"},\n \"fuel_height_accuracy\": {\"value\": {\"min\": -1, \"max\": 1}, \"unit_symbol\": \"mm\"},\n \"temperature_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": -25, \"max\": 55}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"},\n \"material_by_probe_type\": {\n   \"axis\": \"probe type\",\n   \"values\": [\n     {\"condition\": \"Petro-Stik\", \"value\": {\"value\": \"Stainless steel\", \"unit_symbol\": null}},\n     {\"condition\": \"Flex-Stik\", \"value\": {\"value\": \"PVDF\", \"unit_symbol\": null}}\n   ]\n }\n}\n\nConsole component characteristics:\n{\n \"display_resolution\": {\"value\": 0.1, \"unit_symbol\": \"mm\"},\n \"ambient_temperature\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 0, \"max\": 50}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"},\n \"supply_voltage\": {\"value\": 230, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"},\n \"supply_voltage_tolerance\": {\"value\": \"-15%/+10%\"}\n}\n\nFootnotes: none.\n\nMatrix tables: none.\n\ncharacteristics type_level (whole-cert): maybe none needed since everything is component-level. Could add \"optional_features\". I'll skip characteristics top-level or include minimal. I'll omit top-level characteristics since all data is in components. Hmm, but schema expects characteristics section if present; it's optional (\"Omit sections that are not present\"). The characteristics data IS present but organized under components. That's fine.\n\nShould I also include model_family? I'll include it for the probe variants to be safe? The instruction: include ONLY if cert covers >1 model variant. The certified pattern includes 4 probe variants and 1 console. I'll include model_family with models listing the four probes? That could misrepresent since the certified type is the combination. I think components+config capture it better. But strictly, cert covers multiple model variants of probes. I'll include:\n\n\"model_family\": {\n  \"family_name\": null,\n  \"models\": [\n    {\"model_id\": \"AMETEK Petro-Stik 7100\"},\n    {\"model_id\": \"AMETEK Petro-Stik 7250\"},\n    {\"model_id\": \"AMETEX 7100V Flex-Stik\"},\n    {\"model_id\": \"AMETEX 7250V Flex-Stik\"}\n  ]\n}\n\nHmm, and then material as model_level:\n{\"attribute\": \"probe_material\", \"values\": [\n  {\"model\": \"AMETEK Petro-Stik 7100\", \"value\": {\"value\": \"Stainless steel\"}},\n  {\"model\": \"AMETEK Petro-Stik 7250\", \"value\": {\"value\": \"Stainless steel\"}},\n  {\"model\": \"AMETEX 7100V Flex-Stik\", \"value\": {\"value\": \"PVDF\"}},\n  {\"model\": \"AMETEX 7250V Flex-Stik\", \"value\": {\"value\": \"PVDF\"}}\n]}\n\nWait — the structured value for string values: {\"value\": \"Stainless steel\", \"unit_symbol\": null}. In model_level values, the shape is {model, value, footnote_markers} per example, where value can be number. For R137 example they used plain numbers. For strings I'll use {\"model\": ..., \"value\": \"Stainless steel\"} — fine.\n\nActually, wait: \"AMETEX 7100V & 7250V Flex- Stik\" — likely \"AMETEK 7100V & 7250V Flex-Stik\" but OCR says AMETEX. Keep as extracted: \"AMETEX 7100V\" & \"AMETEX 7250V\". I'll keep \"AMETEX\" as in source.\n\nAlso note the certificate revision history first row: \"R85/2008-GB1-11.01\", 28 June 2011, \"Type approval first issued\". Revision: original — I'll use \"0\". Second: \"Revision 1\", 20 May 2015, changes \"Table 1, Flex Stik probes added.\"\n\nCertificate revision: \"1\".\n\nDate format: keep as \"20 May 2015\" or ISO? Schema doesn't specify; I'll use ISO \"2015-05-20\" — safer to keep original? I'll use \"2015-05-20\" as it's a date field. Hmm, examples not given. Many implementations keep raw strings. I'll use ISO.\n\nRevision history dates: \"2011-06-28\", \"2015-05-20\".\n\nPhone: \"+44 (0) 20 8943 7272\". Include.\n\nmember_state